      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59191,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 232,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 400,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 233,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59192,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 233,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours arise in the deep soft tissue, with occasional exceptions {26504053}, most often in the lower extremity but also in the chest/abdominal wall, upper extremity, abdomen/retroperitoneum, and head and neck {26504053; 11406662; 8333559}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 401,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 234,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59193,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 234,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients present with rapidly growing, usually deep, variably painful soft tissue swelling {11406662}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 402,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 235,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59194,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 235,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are tumours of adults and are most common in the sixth to seventh decades of life (mean age: ~72&nbsp;years), occurring more frequently in men than in women, with an M:F&nbsp;ratio of 1.8:1 {26504053}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 403,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 236,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59195,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 236,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 404,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 237,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59196,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 237,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcomas have complex karyotypes with numerical and unbalanced structural changes but no recurrent structural alterations {19480930}. Genome-wide surveys have identified recurrent losses of DNA (e.g. 10q23), gains (e.g. 1p22-p23), and amplifications {12550764}. This copy-number pattern differs from that found in either alveolar or embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma. The genetic profiles of pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcomas are indistinguishable from those of undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcomas {30709449}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 411,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 244,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59197,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 244,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pleomorphic rhabdomyosarcomas are usually large, well-marginated tumours {26504053}, with a pseudocapsule and a whitish or fleshy cut surface, often with necrosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 405,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 238,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59198,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 238,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are composed of sheets of large, atypical, and frequently multinucleated polygonal, spindled, or rhabdoid cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm. Tumours typically display strong desmin expression and often limited expression of MYOD1 and myogenin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 406,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 239,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59199,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 239,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 407,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 240,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59200,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 240,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 410,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 243,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59201,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 243,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> pleomorphic cells with copious brightly eosinophilic cytoplasm; immunolabelling for desmin and myogenin.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 408,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 241,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59202,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 241,
        "headingText": "<p>See <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/33/104\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Embryonal rhabdomyosarcoma</em>.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-02-19T15:51:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 409,
    "bookid": 33,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 242,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 59203,
        "bookId": 33,
        "chapterId": 107,
        "headingId": 242,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are highly aggressive sarcomas. The median survival was 7.3&nbsp;months in the largest study to date, and about 80% of patients died of disease {26504053}. Patients with superficial tumours (~20%) had a favourable outcome. Metastases to lungs are common {26504053; 11406662; 8333559}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-12-11T11:56:48"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]